menu "ARTS camera sync"

    choice ARTS_SYNC_RUN_MODE
        prompt "Run mode"
        default ARTS_SYNC_MODE_SELFTEST
        help
            Which of the Stage 5 bring-up rungs this build serves. BLINK and SELFTEST keep
            the UART console and do not start micro-ROS; MICROROS takes UART0 for the
            micro-ROS transport, which means the console must be disabled and printf
            debugging is no longer available.

        config ARTS_SYNC_MODE_BLINK
            bool "blink (Stage 5a) — slow visible pulse on an LED, software timed"
        config ARTS_SYNC_MODE_SELFTEST
            bool "selftest (Stage 5b/5d) — real trigger + capture, results printed to console"
        config ARTS_SYNC_MODE_MICROROS
            bool "micro-ROS (Stage 5e) — publish CameraSyncPulse, console disabled"
    endchoice

    config ARTS_SYNC_CAMERA_COUNT
        int "Number of camera channels"
        range 1 3
        default 2
        help
            Capture channels consumed = this value plus one if the trigger loopback is
            enabled, and MCPWM group 0 has only three. Two cameras plus the loopback is the
            current build; a third camera requires dropping the loopback.

    config ARTS_SYNC_TRIGGER_LOOPBACK
        bool "Capture the trigger edge via loopback"
        default y
        help
            Wire the trigger output back into the loopback pin so the trigger edge is
            timestamped on the SAME counter as the exposure strobes. This is what makes the
            absolute trigger-to-exposure delay meaningful. Without it, camera 0 becomes the
            reference and only inter-camera skew is measurable — which is still the metric
            the paper reports, so dropping it to free a channel for a third camera is an
            acceptable trade.

    config ARTS_SYNC_RATE_HZ
        int "Trigger rate (Hz)"
        range 10 200
        default 30
        help
            Below 10 Hz the MCPWM period register (16-bit at 1 MHz) overflows; the BLINK
            mode uses software timing precisely so it can go slower and stay visible.
            30 Hz is the stereo default: two 3072x2048 Bayer8 streams at 40 Hz exceed the
            usable bandwidth of one 5 Gbps USB3 link.

    config ARTS_SYNC_PULSE_WIDTH_US
        int "Trigger pulse width (us)"
        range 20 10000
        default 100
        help
            Must comfortably exceed the opto-isolated input's 18 us low-to-high
            propagation delay. 100 us is ~0.3 % duty at 30 Hz.

    config ARTS_SYNC_STROBE_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "ExposureActive strobe is active low"
        default n
        help
            Which edge of the strobe marks the START of exposure. Both edges are captured
            either way, so this decides labelling only.

            Default is n, i.e. active HIGH, and that is a MEASURED result rather than a
            derivation. On BFS-U3-63S4C (firmware 1801.0.9.0) the opto-coupled Line 1 sits
            LOW against the external 1 kohm pull-up and rises while exposing. That is the
            opposite of what "open-collector output" leads you to expect, and this option
            defaulted to y on the strength of that expectation until rung 5d contradicted
            it on 2026-08-18: with a 5000 us exposure and a 379 us TriggerDelay the falling
            edge landed at 5687 us, which is the end of the exposure, not the start.

            The setting is now self-checking. Read exposure_width_ticks in SELFTEST: it
            should equal the configured exposure time. If it instead comes out as the
            trigger period minus the exposure, this option is set the wrong way round and
            every trigger->exposure figure is really a trigger->exposure-END figure.

    config ARTS_SYNC_EDGE_DIAG
        bool "Print every anomalous strobe record (SELFTEST only)"
        depends on ARTS_SYNC_MODE_SELFTEST
        default n
        help
            Logs, for any pulse with a missing start, an edge count other than two, or an
            edge whose reported type disagrees with the pin level, every capture event as
            the ISR saw it: reported edge, pin level, ticks from the trigger. This is the
            instrument that found the mislabelled-edge defect on 2026-10-28; re-run it after
            any ESP-IDF bump or board change.

endmenu
